      "title": "COVENANT compiler architecture prevents workflow misalignment in LLM agents",
      "url": "http://arxiv.org/abs/2607.25400v1",
      "utility_score": 9,
      "why_read": "This text introduces COVENANT, a novel compiler-interpreter architecture that prevents LLM agents from misaligning with complex workflow instructions. Readers will learn how treating workflow instructions as source programs can significantly improve agent reliability and execution success.",
      "content": "A major headache with LLM agents is workflow misalignment: agents skipping steps, taking unsupported branches, or misusing tools, even when given clear natural-language instructions. They interpret prompts, but do not execute them rigorously.\n\nCOVENANT proposes a radical solution: treat workflow instructions not as prompts, but as source programs. It compiles these instructions into an abstract syntax tree (WAST) and a control-flow graph (WCFG). Then, a runtime controller interprets the WCFG, validating every proposed agent action against the explicit workflow requirements.\n\nThis compiler-and-interpreter architecture significantly boosts benchmark success from 50 percent to 83.33 percent and cuts workflow misalignment by 62.75 percent relative. It offers a structured, auditable way to enforce process adherence, moving agent alignment from \"hopeful prompting\" to \"guaranteed execution.\"\n\nThis is a paradigm shift for anyone designing production-grade LLM agents. It provides a robust blueprint for building agents that are not only capable, but also reliably compliant with your business logic."
