      "why_read": "This article reveals that simple blind sampling strategies, such as random uniform or cluster sampling, are more effective and performant than complex proxy-guided methods for data quality profiling at scale. Readers will learn which sampling methods to use for production-grade quality monitoring and understand the reasons behind their superior performance.",
      "content": "Most data quality pipelines rely on progressive sampling, but which strategy actually works best at scale? This benchmark of nine methods reveals a surprising truth: simple random uniform sampling often crushes more complex, proxy-guided approaches.\n\nFor typical data-centric AI pipelines, blind representative samplers achieve near-linear performance (O(N^0.964)) and deliver significantly lower mean relative error (0.49%) compared to methods like DAG-guided MCMC, which can be 11-49 times worse and run super-linearly.\n\nThe key takeaway? Representativeness matters more than domain knowledge in sampling for quality profiling. Do not overcomplicate your sampling; a schema-free random uniform approach might be your best bet for production-grade scale."
